{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1516142371,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE0MjcxOTI5MzI="},"added":["enhancement"],"removed":[]},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1516142371,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE0MjcxOTI5MzM="},"added":["UX"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1516182739,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM1ODI1MjkyMA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2084#issuecomment-358252920"},"message":"I know not everyone is a fan of this, but I would propose to implement such helper as a plugin. Doing this as part of the aggregation routine is probably a lot slower as it will actually have to handle all the extracted metadata, merge and filter things. In this context we actually do not want any filters are merges (as they would shadow which particular parser provided a piece of metadata), but simply loop over all parsers and reports the statistic described above.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1516187490,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM1ODI3Mjk1Nw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2084#issuecomment-358272957"},"message":"\u003e parsers are not really exposed well to the user, so it is impossible to know (at a level or user API) which parsers are available\n\nWell, this could easily be solved by providing `metadata --get-available-parsers`, which would spit out the name to use as a reference in config and may be a description if provided by the parser.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1518254681,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2NDYzODQ2NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2084#issuecomment-364638465"},"message":"@yarikoptic mentioned that he wanted to cook up some autodetection tool","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1519327584,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2Nzc5MjI0Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2085#issuecomment-367792242"},"message":"I played with this a little and looked at the amount of code that needs to run to get a proper command implementation hooked into the python or the cmdline API. Bottom line, there is not much (after I discovered that #2214 is possible). SO what I outlined above is doable: when can kill the `plugin` command and make any plugin a real command, we just need to add the ability to search a few dedicated places for commands, instead of hard-coding an exhaustive list.\n\nQ remains: how to we deal with the lookup in order to avoid unnecessary import/startup delays. For the cmdline interface, it should be relatively easy to load a shim on startup that has just the name and a one-line synopsis on a command. However, I don't have a good idea on how to achieve a similar effect for the python api. The latter is used throughout the entire code base, meaning that any command using anything from the API, causes all of the API to be built -- which makes any/most optimizations done at the cmdline end more or less pointless.\n\nAny ideas?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1519330453,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2NzgwNzY0Nw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2085#issuecomment-367807647"},"message":"Ok, 40min no response -- clearly nobody cares....\n\nSo here is the master plan:\n\n1. `api` -\u003e `coreapi`\n   This will then be pruned of any command that is not super-important (or a dependency of a super-important command). This new coreapi will then be loaded the same way as we do now (hardcoded list of imports)\n2. new `api`\n    This will be all the rest, and will be looked up from a configurable number of locations. No hardcoded list but dynamic lookup. Any `coreapi` command must not use any functionality from `api`. `api` will become the defacto replacement of `plugin`. `api` will likely `from coreapi import *` for simplicity on the user side.\n3. cmdline API will load all of `coreapi` the usual way, and discover commands in `api` dynamically, while including only a shim in the argparse instance, than will turn itself into an actual command if executed -- that latter part sound good, and should be doable, but I have no crisp idea on how to do it exactly.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1519331393,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2NzgxMTk0Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2085#issuecomment-367811946"},"message":"On (3): maybe we can just hand-parse `sys.argv` first, and unless there is `--help` or an alias of it, we only load commands whose name appears somewhere in the arg list.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1519331548,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2NzgxMjcxNw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2085#issuecomment-367812717"},"message":"I would say -- pregenerate (and keep within code tree, e.g. `datalad/api_generated.py` or alike) for all commands/plugins we ship, have a unittest to verify that pregenerated structure is up-to-date happen changes happen.  Use that structure within cmdline (and may be even api.py if makes sense?)\nAll the dynamically discovered (if there would be any) could be analyzed etc on the fly -- if there is none (majority of cases), no impact.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1519331934,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2NzgxNDUyMw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2085#issuecomment-367814523"},"message":"I don't like that somehow. We would ship pregenerated content that somehow needs to be loaded, reformated to terminal size, etc -- just to save a few ms on `--help`?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1519332714,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2NzgxNzkwMA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2085#issuecomment-367817900"},"message":"Tried a quick hack that still loads the command names, but only builds the subparsers for command if they are mentioned in the cmdline. Brings the startup time down from \u003e250ms to about 80ms.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1519340637,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2Nzg1MzEwMA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2085#issuecomment-367853100"},"message":"sweet!  that is 80ms for running any command or just plain `datalad`?\nfor me it takes 100ms only to import datalad:\n```shell\n$\u003e time python -c 'import datalad'\npython -c 'import datalad'  0.09s user 0.02s system 97% cpu 0.109 total\n```","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1519352367,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2Nzg4OTI3MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2085#issuecomment-367889270"},"message":"I am not sure if an answer to any question is still awaited or magical resolution is being cooked already per few comments back... indeed if we could avoid importing and pre-propulating all subparsers for a specific command, it could provide a remedy I guess.  For `datalad --help` we could load them all.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1519364306,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2NzkxNTI0Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2085#issuecomment-367915242"},"message":"Import datalad is/was quick. The 80ms referred to datalad startup via the command line. which was \u003e250ms for me.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1519502534,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2ODI1NTYwOA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2085#issuecomment-368255608"},"message":"Soooo, I found the big showstopper. The beast that is `eval_func` makes very sophisticated assumptions on the origin and naming of classes, and modules they are coming from. In particular, any command MUST be properly imported so it gets `__module__`. For dynamically loaded code there is no `__module__`. @bpoldrack might remember what led to this solution. I just remember that the way towards it was painful.\n\nIt seems to be doing that in order to figure out which class provided the wrapped command function `__call__`, which in turn is needed to find default parameters etc.\n\nATM I see no easy way forward. Maybe a look dict (wrapped function id: class) could be managed somewhere. But right now I am at a loss where and when this information exists and could be captured.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1521531353,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM3NDUwMjI3Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2085#issuecomment-374502276"},"message":"Done now.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1521531353,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MTUzMDIzNzM1Mw=="},"status":2}]}